The prohibitions of this ordinance shall not apply to the following sources of noise:

- (a) Noises and/or sounds caused to be made by manufacturing, industrial, governmental, or commercial entities in the normal course of their business;

- (b) —_ Noises and/or sounds emanating from any official event on property owned by a federal, state, or local government, or any official event on Hall County School District or private school property;

- (c) Noises and/or sounds that are permitted by a license or permit issued by the Town; provided, however, that the producer or coordinator of the event must strictly comply with the terms, restrictions and conditions of the permit;

- (d) Noises or sounds made by birds and animals;

- (e) Noises or sounds normally made by cars, trucks, trains, airplanes, helicopters, buses and similar vehicles, when properly equipped with noise-limiting devices required by law;

- (f) Sound volumes produced by radio, CD or tape player, or other mechanical sound making device or instrument from within a motor vehicle while on a public street or highway, which sound is controlled by the O.C.G.A. § 40-6-14;

- (g) | Noises or sounds from property maintenance activity conducted on or within 100 feet of any residential dwelling unit between the hours of 8:00 a.m, and 8:00 p.m.;

- (h) Noises or sounds from construction activities conducted on or within 100 feet of a property zoned for single-family or multi-family residential use between the hours of 6:30 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. (does not apply within commercial or mixed use zoning classifications);

- (i) Noises or sounds made by law enforcement and other public safety officials performing their public functions; or

- (j) Lawful ignition or discharge of fireworks during days and times authorized by state law.

Where a partial exemption under this section imposes a day or time limitation, a variance from the above-referenced hours of operation may be requested, in writing, at least 48 hours prior to a proposed event, activity, or operation, for consideration by the Town Mayor. Such a request shall state:

- (a) The reasons that support a claim of urgent need based on specific loss or inconvenience for such a variation from the allowable work hours;

- (b) The impact that the denial of this request would have on the applicant's activity, project or event, and the surrounding properties;

- (c) The steps which have been taken by the applicant to communicate those needs and impacts to owners of surrounding and nearby properties;

- (d) The steps that have or will be taken to limit the impact of the proposed activity upon surrounding and nearby properties; and

- (e) The possible risks to public health and safety.

If the Town Mayor finds that the application adequately demonstrates the urgent need for a variance from the above allowable work hours, adequately provides for mitigation of the impact upon surrounding and nearby properties, and poses no additional risk to public health and safety, then permission shall be granted for a variance to alter the allowable work hours during one ten-day period.

The prohibitions of this section shall not apply to government road, water, sewer, stormwater construction or maintenance projects or to Georgia Department of Transportation road construction or maintenance projects.
